  "strategy": "1.Approachandrationale\nWe will use a **variational approach combined with frequency-domain optimization**. Unlike traditional LP or gradient-based methods, this plan leverages the fact that autocorrelation constraints in frequency-domain resemble band-pass filters. The central idea is to:\n- Transform the problem into an optimization over the sequence's power spectrum (frequency content)\n- Iteratively tune the spectral shape to minimize the AC1 upper bound (which corresponds to reducing the energy concentration in the spectral domain)\n- Automatically derive nonnegative time-domain sequences via inverse Fourier transform\nThis approach is distinct from previous LP and block-coordinate methods by directly manipulating frequency-domain regularities, offering several advantages:\n- More regular solutions that generalize better\n- Elimination of discontinuities typically found in step-function representations\n- Natural handling of long sequences through frequency-domain operations\nThe key bottleneck we address is the nonnegative constraint in time-domain optimization, which is circumvented by combining magnitude-constrained spectral initialization with time-domain clipping.\n\n2.Mathematicalspecification\n- **Representation**: Power spectrum P(\u03c9) defined on [-\u03c0,\u03c0] with Parseval's property \n- **Sequence variables**: Coefficients x(t) \u2208 \u211d\u207a (clipped to [0,1000])\n- **Objective**: Minimize AC1(\u03c9) = 2N \u00d7 min(\u03c9) \u03c9 |P(|\u03c9||)| \u00d7 (sum(x)\u207b\u00b2)\n- **Constraints**: \n  : Time nonnegativity: x(t) \u2265 0 for all t\n  : Normalization: \u222b|x(t)|\u00b2 dt \u2265 0.01\n  : Bandwidth limitation: |P(\u03c9)| \u2264 B(\u03c9) (avoiding extreme frequencies)\n\nImplementation-specific functions:\n- **Fourier transform**: Using `np.fft.irfft` for stable inverses\n- **Magnitude constraint**: |P(\u03c9)| \u2264 1.5 \u00b7 B(\u03c9/R) for \u03c9 \u2264 R, else |P(\u03c9)| \u2264 T\u2080\n- **Spectral initialization**: Use optimal filtering to derive frequency envelopes\n\n3.Implementationprocedure\n- **Phase1: Parametricsearch**\n  1. Initialize with parent sequence at length N=384\n  2. Solve spectral envelope optimization with:\n      - Iterate k=10 times spectral parameters\n      - Update filter coefficients \u03b1(i) \u2190 \u03b2i \u00b7 clip(sin(\u00b7values))\n      - Break after 5% CPU budget\n- **Phase2: Spectral-transformation**\n  1. Construct frequency P(\u03c9) using Burg-algorithm-derived AR model\n  2. Solve LP in time-domain using `linprog`:\n      x \u2190 np.clip(np.abs(irfft(S)),0,1000)\n      constraints=[\u2264 sum_time] appropriately\n  3. Compute AC1 via exact convsply convolution\n- **Phase3: Coordinate-descent**\n  1. For 30% of budget: Localsearch amplitude variations\n  2. Use projected gradient in frequency-domain\n  3. Return sequence with 0.0\u2264x\u1d62\u22641000\n\nDetailedGradientSelection:\n- Use exponential smoothing (\u03b3=0.8) for filter coefficients\n- Convolutioncomputation: FFT of length O(N log N)\n- Stopwhen: \u2202AC1/\u2202\u03b8\u2096 < \u03b5 or score stabilizes\n\n4.Searchchoicesandusefulvariations\n- Default optimization:\n  frequency-bandwidth: [0.1,0.5], high-pass filter\n  spectral-gradient: lbfgs with tight tolerance\n- Threeinitializationvariations:\n  A. Parent(seek-refinement): 1x longer\n  B. Random-harmonic (effective stabilization)\n  C. Multistart with multiple spectral components\n- 5Dynamicfiltersettings: adjust bandpass based on current AC1\n\n5.Computebudgetandrobustness\n- FFT costs: 3\u00d7O(N log N) per evaluation\n- Timeallocation:\n  Parameterratiation: 40% budget\n  Fourier-reversion: 35%\n  Finalrefinement: 25%\n- Costestimates: 0.5 seconds for N=384\n  \u2192 4096-point FFT \u2248 8\u00d7 cheaper than dense methods\n- Stallingcriteria:\n  score_improvements < 5 epochs \u2192 restart variation\n  Normalizegradients\u2192 epsilon-safe implementation\n\n6.Validationandreturncontract\n- Revalidatefinalcandidatewith`evaluate_sequence`\n- Fallback: Return project(random_parent_lowest_score)\n- Scorenormalization: 0.01<sum(x)<1000",
